## 10. KNOWLEDGE GRAPH — MAKE IT A SIGNATURE EXPERIENCE

The Knowledge Graph should be one of the most visually distinctive parts of the website.

Use the existing graph technology and preserve its data relationships.

### Visual direction

- Elegant dark-brown or neutral canvas where appropriate.
- Gold and cream node accents.
- Clear node hierarchy.
- Fine connection lines.
- Soft highlight effects.
- Subtle node animations.
- Smooth zoom and pan.
- Selected-node emphasis.
- Clear labels that remain readable.
- A clean legend.
- A modern side panel for selected-node details.

### Interactions

- Click a node to select it.
- Hover or focus to reveal concise information.
- Click a connected node to explore relationships.
- Expand neighboring nodes on demand.
- Search for a museum or subject.
- Filter by node type.
- Reset the view.
- Zoom in and out.
- Drag the graph canvas.
- Support keyboard-accessible alternatives where practical.
- Show details in a side panel or responsive drawer.
- Provide a clear path back to the directory or museum detail page.

### Performance

- Do not render all nodes simultaneously if the dataset is large.
- Use lazy loading or neighborhood expansion.
- Avoid excessive continuous animation.
- Avoid rebuilding the entire graph on every interaction.
- Provide a loading state and an empty state.
- Preserve the existing relationship semantics.

Use only relationships supported by the data.

Do not create fictional connections to make the graph look more impressive.

